WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.888
Practical GPU cost control starts before a workload runs.

00:00:03.888 --> 00:00:07.677
Classify the workload, define its cost intent, assign ownership,

00:00:07.677 --> 00:00:12.728
and choose controls that match the value and risk of the work.

00:00:12.728 --> 00:00:14.833
Experiments, training jobs, batch evaluations,

00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:18.200
and production inference do not need identical policies.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:22.409
Choose the smallest suitable resource, measure utilization, and scale deliberately.

00:00:22.409 --> 00:00:25.777
Use queues, schedules, shutdown policies, timeouts, retry limits,

00:00:25.777 --> 00:00:29.986
and cleanup automation to reduce idle time and runaway execution.

00:00:29.986 --> 00:00:33.353
Revisit right-sizing as models, data, and traffic change.

00:00:33.353 --> 00:00:38.405
Manage the costs around the GPU as carefully as the GPU itself.

00:00:38.405 --> 00:00:42.614
Storage, checkpoints, logs, artifacts, snapshots, caches, data movement, failed jobs,

00:00:42.614 --> 00:00:45.560
and operational labor all affect effective cost.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:51.033
Hidden spend becomes easier to control when ownership and retention rules are explicit.

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:53.558
Finally, make cost visible and reviewable.

00:00:53.558 --> 00:00:56.926
Budgets, tags, quotas, guardrails, dashboards, anomaly alerts, forecasts,

00:00:56.926 --> 00:01:01.556
and regular reviews help teams connect engineering decisions to financial outcomes.

00:01:01.556 --> 00:01:05.344
Sustainable savings come from repeatable habits, not one-time cleanup.

00:01:05.344 --> 00:01:09.133
When every workload has intent, ownership, limits, and review,

00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:12.500
cloud GPU cost becomes manageable instead of mysterious.
